[{"id":"29571","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"768","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span style=\"font-weight:bold\">What are the products in a reaction where an acid reacts with a base?</span></p>","option_1":"Acid and water","option_2":"Base and water","option_3":"Salt and water","option_4":"Gas and water","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"3","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>Correct Answer: Option C - Salt and water.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>This is a neutralisation reaction: Acid + Base → Salt + Water. For example, HCl + NaOH → NaCl (salt) + H2O (water). The acid's H+ ions combine with the base's OH- ions to form water, while the remaining ions form a salt.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29572","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"768","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span style=\"font-weight:bold\">Training of Rural Youth for Self-Employment (TRYSEM) was initiated in which year?</span></p>","option_1":"1965","option_2":"1971","option_3":"1979","option_4":"1983","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"3","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>Correct Answer: Option C - 1979.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>TRYSEM (Training of Rural Youth for Self-Employment) was launched by the Government of India in 1979 to provide technical and entrepreneurial skills to rural youth (aged 18-35) from families below the poverty line, enabling them to take up self-employment ventures. It was later merged into the Swarnajayanti Gram Swarozgar Yojana (SGSY) in 1999. This question was left unanswered by the candidate.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29573","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"768","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span style=\"font-weight:bold\">Which among the following gland in human body is located in lowermost part of body?</span></p>","option_1":"Pineal gland","option_2":"Pituitary gland","option_3":"Thyroid gland","option_4":"Adrenal gland","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"4","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>Correct Answer: Option D - Adrenal gland.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>Among the glands listed, the Adrenal gland (located atop each kidney, in the abdominal region) is positioned lowest in the body. The Pineal and Pituitary glands are both located in the brain, and the Thyroid gland is located in the neck - all of which are higher up in the body compared to the adrenal glands near the kidneys.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29574","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"768","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span style=\"font-weight:bold\">Who among the following kings acquired the title of 'Dakshinapatheshvara' (lord of the south)?</span></p>","option_1":"Upagupta","option_2":"Kharavela","option_3":"Pulakesin II","option_4":"Mahapadma Nanda","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"3","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>Correct Answer: Option C - Pulakesin II.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>Pulakesin II, the greatest ruler of the Chalukya dynasty of Badami, famously defeated the Pallavas and other southern rulers and even repelled an invasion by the north Indian emperor Harshavardhana. He assumed the title 'Dakshinapatheshvara' (Lord of the South/Deccan) to signify his supremacy over the southern region.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29575","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"768","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span style=\"font-weight:bold\">'Jhanjhar' is a popular dance of which of the following Indian state?</span></p>","option_1":"Jharkhand","option_2":"Madhya Pradesh","option_3":"Maharashtra","option_4":"Himachal Pradesh","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"4","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>Correct Answer: Option D - Himachal Pradesh.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>Jhanjhar (also called Jhinjhoti/Jhanjhar) is a folk dance performed in Himachal Pradesh, particularly in the Kangra and Shimla regions, typically performed by women during festive and social occasions, often accompanied by traditional folk music and ankle bells.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29576","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"768","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span style=\"font-weight:bold\">The 'International Kite Festival' in Gujarat coincides with which festival?</span></p>","option_1":"Holi","option_2":"Diwali","option_3":"Krishna Janmashtami","option_4":"Makar Sankranti","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"4","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>Correct Answer: Option D - Makar Sankranti.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>Gujarat's International Kite Festival (Uttarayan) is held annually around 14 January in Ahmedabad and other cities, coinciding with Makar Sankranti - the festival that marks the sun's transit into Capricorn and the beginning of longer days, traditionally celebrated by flying kites.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29577","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"768","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span style=\"font-weight:bold\">Who was one of Sirajuddaulah's commander that played a significant role in the Sirajuddaulah's defeat at the Battle of Plassey?</span></p>","option_1":"Alivardi Khan","option_2":"Mir Jafar","option_3":"Mulla Qutb Shah","option_4":"Jagat Seth","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"2","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>Correct Answer: Option B - Mir Jafar.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>Mir Jafar, the commander-in-chief of Siraj-ud-Daulah's army, betrayed the Nawab by secretly conspiring with the British East India Company (led by Robert Clive) ahead of the Battle of Plassey (1757). His treachery - withholding his troops from fighting - was decisive in Siraj-ud-Daulah's defeat, after which the British installed Mir Jafar as the new Nawab of Bengal. Alivardi Khan was Siraj's predecessor (grandfather), not a commander at Plassey.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29578","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"768","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span style=\"font-weight:bold\">Which of the following gas is used as fuels in vehicles these days?</span></p>","option_1":"Compressed natural gas","option_2":"Nitrogen gas","option_3":"Liquefied petroleum gas","option_4":"Helium gas","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"1","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>Correct Answer: Option A - Compressed natural gas.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>Compressed Natural Gas (CNG), primarily composed of methane, is widely used as a cleaner-burning alternative fuel for vehicles, especially in public transport and private cars in India, due to lower emissions compared to petrol/diesel. While LPG is also used in some vehicles, CNG is the most common and widely promoted vehicular fuel gas in the current Indian context; Nitrogen and Helium are inert gases not used as fuels.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29579","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"768","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span style=\"font-weight:bold\">The International Space Station is a large space station in low earth orbit at about _____ Km from the Earth surface.</span></p>","option_1":"400","option_2":"1000","option_3":"5000","option_4":"22000","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"1","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>Correct Answer: Option A - 400.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>The International Space Station (ISS) orbits the Earth at an altitude of approximately 400 km (about 250 miles), completing one orbit roughly every 90 minutes. This is classified as Low Earth Orbit (LEO), much lower than geostationary orbit (~36,000 km) used by many communication satellites.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29580","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"768","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span style=\"font-weight:bold\">The total number of women contesting general elections in the country has increased to _____ in 2024.</span></p>","option_1":"10%","option_2":"14%","option_3":"16%","option_4":"21%","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"1","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>Correct Answer: Option A - 10%.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>As per data on the 2024 Lok Sabha general elections, the proportion of women candidates contesting increased to around 10% of the total candidates - still a relatively small share, reflecting the continuing gender gap in electoral political participation in India, though there has been gradual improvement over previous elections.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29581","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"768","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span style=\"font-weight:bold\">'FireSat', a satellite put into orbit by Elon Musk's SpaceX, is backed by which tech company?</span></p>","option_1":"Apple","option_2":"Google","option_3":"Amazon","option_4":"Infosys","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"2","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>Correct Answer: Option B - Google.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>FireSat is a satellite constellation project backed by Google, in partnership with the Earth Fire Alliance, designed to detect wildfires early using specialised sensors, with the satellites launched into orbit using SpaceX's Falcon 9 rockets.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29582","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"768","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span style=\"font-weight:bold\">Why does a paper cup not burn when filled with water and heated?</span></p>","option_1":"The water evaporates and cools the paper","option_2":"The water absorbs the heat, preventing the paper from reaching its ignition temperature","option_3":"The paper becomes fireproof when wet","option_4":"The water creates steam, which extinguishes the flame","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"2","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>Correct Answer: Option B - The water absorbs the heat, preventing the paper from reaching its ignition temperature.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>When a paper cup filled with water is heated, the water absorbs the heat energy (since water has a high specific heat capacity), keeping the paper's temperature at or near the boiling point of water (100°C) - well below paper's ignition temperature (around 230°C). As long as water remains in the cup to absorb the heat, the paper does not reach a temperature high enough to catch fire. This question was left unanswered by the candidate.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29583","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"768","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span style=\"font-weight:bold\">Which of the following economic activity is included in the primary sector?</span></p>","option_1":"Trade and logistics","option_2":"Healthcare","option_3":"Manufacturing","option_4":"Fishing","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"4","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>Correct Answer: Option D - Fishing.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>The primary sector includes economic activities directly involving the extraction or harvesting of natural resources, such as agriculture, fishing, forestry, and mining. Trade and logistics and Healthcare belong to the tertiary (services) sector, while Manufacturing belongs to the secondary sector.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29584","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"768","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span style=\"font-weight:bold\">India qualified for the _____ FIFA World Cup in Brazil but AIFF refused to send a team citing 'disagreements over team selection and insufficient practice time'.</span></p>","option_1":"1942","option_2":"1962","option_3":"1950","option_4":"1954","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"3","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>Correct Answer: Option C - 1950.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>India qualified for the 1950 FIFA World Cup in Brazil by default (after other teams in their qualifying group withdrew), but the All India Football Federation (AIFF) ultimately withdrew the team, citing several reasons commonly reported including disagreements over team selection, lack of practice time, and (as popular legend also states) issues around playing barefoot versus wearing boots, along with the cost and logistics of travel.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29585","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"768","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span style=\"font-weight:bold\">Which of the following activity is responsible for disturbance of the balance between the process of soil formation and soil erosion?</span></p>","option_1":"Deforestation","option_2":"Afforestation","option_3":"Organic farming","option_4":"Intercropping","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"1","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>Correct Answer: Option A - Deforestation.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>Deforestation removes the vegetation cover that protects topsoil from wind and water erosion and that contributes organic matter for soil formation. This disrupts the natural balance between soil formation and soil erosion, leading to faster erosion rates than the soil can regenerate. Afforestation, organic farming, and intercropping, by contrast, generally help conserve soil and maintain this balance.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29586","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"768","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span style=\"font-weight:bold\">The National Capital Goods Policy in India was launched in _____.</span></p>","option_1":"2013","option_2":"2016","option_3":"2019","option_4":"2021","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"2","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>Correct Answer: Option B - 2016.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>The National Capital Goods Policy was launched by the Government of India in 2016 with the aim of increasing the capital goods sector's share in manufacturing output, boosting exports, creating skilled employment, and enhancing technology depth in domestic manufacturing.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29587","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"768","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span style=\"font-weight:bold\">Who is the author of the book 'The Day I Stopped Drinking Milk', a collection of heartfelt episodes for young adults?</span></p>","option_1":"Sudha Murty","option_2":"Chetan Bhagat","option_3":"Anita Desai","option_4":"Samit Basu","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"1","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>Correct Answer: Option A - Sudha Murty.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>'The Day I Stopped Drinking Milk' is authored by Sudha Murty, a prolific Indian author known for her simple, heartfelt storytelling style aimed at young readers, often based on real-life experiences and moral lessons.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","pos